                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Chromatic is a fast arcade game built around a pretty simple idea, using colours to survive waves of enemies in space. I control a Chromacore and have to destroy hazards by choosing the opposite colour, while trying to keep everything moving quickly enough to build up big combos. I like how easy it is to understand because I can start playing almost immediately. There is no complicated story getting in the way, it is mostly about reacting quickly, choosing the right colour and trying to survive for as long as I can.\n\nThe colour mechanic is what makes it more interesting than a completely standard survival arcade game. I have to keep an eye on what is coming towards me and react with the correct colour rather than simply holding down a weapon and shooting everything. The game has more than 50 different hazards that are introduced through increasing difficulty, along with boss fights that give me something more demanding to deal with. I also like the combo system because it gives me another reason to stay accurate instead of only worrying about surviving.\n\nThe pace is probably the biggest strength for me. Things start fairly calmly but become much more intense as the game continues, and I have to react faster as more hazards appear. That gives it a nice arcade feeling where I can finish a run and immediately want to try again to see if I can do better. There are also more than 40 achievements and leaderboards, which suit this kind of game because the main progression is really about improving my own performance and lasting longer.\n\nI also like that there is local cooperative play. It gives the game another way to approach the same basic idea, with a second person able to join in using the Chroma Missile Launcher. I do not think this suddenly turns Chromatic into a much deeper game, but it does make the simple arcade formula more enjoyable when I want something I can play together with someone else. Mouse and gamepad support also give me a choice in how I want to control it.\n\nThe biggest problem for me is that the basic idea can only go so far. Once I understand the colour system, I am still doing essentially the same thing for every run, just with faster enemies and more difficult patterns. The randomised hazards help keep things from being completely predictable, but there is not a huge amount of variety outside the survival loop. I also think the presentation is very colourful and energetic, but it can become visually overwhelming when everything starts happening at once. That is part of the challenge, but it can also make some deaths feel more like I lost track of what was happening than made a clear mistake.\n\nOverall, Chromatic is a fun little arcade game that knows exactly what it wants to be. I like the colour based combat, fast pacing, combo system, boss fights and local co op, and the large number of hazards gives me enough variety to keep improving for a while. At the same time, the core gameplay is very repetitive and there is not enough depth for me to treat it as anything more than a quick arcade experience. It works best when I want something simple that tests my reactions and lets me chase a better run without demanding a huge time commitment. For a free game, I think there is enough here to make it worth trying, but it falls short of the level of variety I would need for one of my higher ratings.\n\nRating: 7.5/10\n",
